/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 46 0 R
/T (Why the merge-once rule holds. In row 0, 2 2 2 becomes 4 2, not 8: after the first two 2s merge into a 4, the scan skips past the partner, so the new 4 is never compared with the third 2. In row 1, 4 4 8 8 becomes 8 16, not 32: the 8 created by the 4s is not merged with the original 8s, which merge only with each other. In row 3, the 4 created from two 2s does not merge with the existing 4.)
/E (Why the merge-once rule holds. In row 0, 2 2 2 becomes 4 2, not 8: after the first two 2s merge into a 4, the scan skips past the partner, so the new 4 is never compared with the third 2. In row 1, 4 4 8 8 becomes 8 16, not 32: the 8 created by the 4s is not merged with the original 8s, which merge only with each other. In row 3, the 4 created from two 2s does not merge with the existing 4.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [16]
>>
